Design templates for AI slide generation
=========================================

Each subdirectory here is the unpacked source of one presentation design
template. The build packs it into a .otp file and installs it into the
share/cool-ai-templates directory. The AI slide assistant offers these
templates in its picker and applies the one the user chooses to a generated
deck.


How templates are discovered
-----------------------------

At run time the engine looks for templates in two places, in this order:

1. The bundled directory share/cool-ai-templates, where the templates in this
   source tree end up.
2. The integrator preset directory share/template/common/presnt.

A template is any file whose name ends in .otp. The base name (the file name
without the .otp suffix) is the name shown in the picker, so it must be at
most 64 characters, each one a plain letter, digit, space, hyphen, or
underscore. A file whose name does not fit is skipped.

When a bundled template and a preset share the same name (compared without
regard to letter case), the bundled one wins and the preset copy is ignored.


How a master slide's role is decided
-------------------------------------

The assistant places each generated slide on the master that suits its part
of the deck: an opening, a divider, a body, or a closing. To do that the
engine has to guess what part each master in the template plays. That guess is
a best-effort heuristic, not a contract you should design your masters around.

The engine works out a master's role in two steps.

First it reads the master's own name and looks for keywords, checked in a
fixed order. It stops at the first group that matches:

1. Divider, if the name contains separator, divider, section, topic, chapter,
   agenda, or transition.
2. Closing, if the name contains ending, closing, thank, farewell, goodbye,
   or finale.
3. Body, if the name contains content, outline, body, or bullet.
4. Title, if the name contains title.

The match is a plain substring match and ignores letter case. The order
matters: many real names carry the word "title" next to a stronger word, so
"Section Title" and "Title and Content Divider" both come out as dividers,
while a plain "Title" or "Subtitle" falls through to the title group last. A
name that matches nothing is left undecided for the next step.

Second, for any master the name did not settle, the engine looks at the
example slides in the template that use that master and reads the layout each
one uses. A title-and-subtitle layout reads as an opening, a title-only or
blank layout as a divider, and anything with body content as a body slide.

Because this is only a heuristic, renaming a master or changing its example
slides can quietly change the role it is given. That is safe: the roles stay
inside the engine and never travel to the model or the browser, so a
reclassification changes only which master a slide lands on, never the
protocol. Do not treat particular master names as a stable way to pin a role.


Declaring roles with a manifest
-------------------------------

A template can state its master roles directly instead of leaving the engine
to guess them. It does this with a manifest: a small JSON document stored in a
standard document property named AIDesignManifest. When a template carries a
manifest, its declared roles take precedence and the name-and-example heuristic
runs only for masters the manifest does not name.

The manifest looks like this:

    {
      "schemaVersion": 1,
      "masters": {
        "Opening master name": "opening",
        "Body master name": "body",
        "Divider master name": "divider",
        "Closing master name": "closing"
      },
      "intents": {
        "quote": "Divider master name"
      },
      "artDirection": "One sentence describing the image style.",
      "budgets": {
        "maxSlides": 20,
        "maxItemsPerBullets": 6,
        "maxItemLength": 200,
        "maxTitleLength": 200
      }
    }

Every field except schemaVersion is optional.

- schemaVersion must be 1 or more. A missing version, an older version, or a
  document that does not parse as JSON is treated as no manifest at all, so the
  heuristic runs as before.
- masters maps a master's name to the part it plays: one of opening, body,
  divider, or closing. A name that does not match a master in the template, or
  a part that is not one of those four words, is ignored.
- intents maps a word the assistant may attach to a slide to the master that
  word should land on. The keys are lowercase letters and hyphens, up to
  thirty-two characters. A value that does not name a master in the template is
  ignored. This lets a template send, say, a quote slide to a particular master
  without the assistant ever learning the master's name.
- artDirection is one sentence describing the image style. It is used only to
  compose image-generation prompts, never the chat prompt.
- budgets are upper limits the assistant keeps within. They may only tighten
  the server's own limits, never raise them, and a value below one is ignored.

The roles the assistant infers are reported back to you as a source field on
each master, reading declared when the part came from the manifest and inferred
when it came from the heuristic. Use it to check your manifest was read.


Editing and keeping the manifest
--------------------------------

The manifest is an ordinary document property, so you edit it in the
File - Properties dialog, on the Custom Properties tab, in a property named
AIDesignManifest. It survives an ordinary edit and save round-trip. Note that
the "Reset Properties" button in that dialog deletes user-defined properties,
so it removes the manifest as well.
